---
import manifestIllustration from '../assets/control-hierarchy/manifest.svg';
import panelIllustration from '../assets/control-hierarchy/panel.svg';
import pluginIllustration from '../assets/control-hierarchy/plugin.svg';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const install = `pnpm add -D @wesflo/vite-plugin-lockxy`;
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---

<DocsLayout
    title="Overview"
    description="Plug-and-play local API mocks for Vite, with optional manifests and browser controls."
    activePath="/"
>
    <article>
        <p class="kicker">Lockxy · Local Mock Proxy</p>
        <h1>Drop in mock files. Start Vite. Done.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
            Add the plugin and place local response files below your mock directory. Requests are matched automatically
            through naming conventions—no manifest, browser panel, or application-side fetch wrapper is required.
        </p>

        <section class="layer-section" aria-labelledby="layered-control">
            <h2 id="layered-control">Start simple. Add control only when you need it.</h2>
            <p>
                Lockxy follows a bottom-up model that grows with your use case without complicating the basic setup. File
                conventions form the independent foundation, a source-controlled manifest adds deterministic project
                behavior, and the proxy panel adds local convenience. Each layer is optional and never turns the layer
                below it into a hidden dependency.
            </p>
            <ol class="layer-stack">
                <li>
                    <img class="layer-illustration" src={pluginIllustration.src} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <div class="layer-copy">
                        <h3>1. Plugin</h3>
                        <strong>Plugin with file conventions</strong>
                        <p>
                            Always works on its own. Matching requests resolve local response files through
                            URL-to-filename conventions, without requiring a manifest or panel.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <img class="layer-illustration" src={manifestIllustration.src} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <div class="layer-copy">
                        <h3>2. Manifest</h3>
                        <strong>Source-controlled project configuration</strong>
                        <p>
                            Optionally adds explicit routes, response details, and fixed decisions. The configuration
                            remains reproducible without browser state or a panel.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <img class="layer-illustration" src={panelIllustration.src} alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
                    <div class="layer-copy">
                        <h3>3. Proxy panel</h3>
                        <strong>Convenient browser controls</strong>
                        <p>
                            Optionally exposes the available choices and stores temporary browser preferences. It controls
                            only what the manifest has not already fixed and is never required by the plugin.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <section class="value-section" aria-labelledby="why-lockxy">
            <h2 id="why-lockxy">Why teams use Lockxy</h2>
            <ul class="value-list">
                <li>
                    <span class="value-icon" aria-hidden="true">⚡</span>
                    <div>
                        <strong>Plug and play</strong>
                        <p>
                            Add the Vite plugin, drop response files into <code>mock/</code>, and keep using the
                            application's real API calls. Naming conventions do the routing.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="value-icon" aria-hidden="true">✈️</span>
                    <div>
                        <strong>Work fully offline</strong>
                        <p>
                            Keep developing on a plane, on a train, or anywhere without a backend, VPN, internet
                            connection, or expensive hotspot.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="value-icon" aria-hidden="true">🔀</span>
                    <div>
                        <strong>Mocks live with the code</strong>
                        <p>
                            Responses are versioned and reviewed in the repository, so frontend and backend developers can
                            inspect shared API assumptions and diagnose new or parallel endpoints.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="value-icon" aria-hidden="true">🔒</span>
                    <div>
                        <strong>Mock data stays local</strong>
                        <p>
                            Lockxy handles mock files on the developer's machine. Requests and responses are not uploaded
                            to an external mock service.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="value-icon" aria-hidden="true">🪙</span>
                    <div>
                        <strong>No service costs</strong>
                        <p>
                            There is no hosted mock API, usage plan, or metered traffic to pay for—regardless of how often
                            the team runs its local mocks.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <span class="value-icon" aria-hidden="true">🛠️</span>
                    <div>
                        <strong>No hosting overhead</strong>
                        <p>
                            No extra server needs to be provisioned, secured, monitored, or maintained just to support
                            local frontend development.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <Code code={install} label="terminal" />

        <div class="cards">
            <a class="card" href={`${base}/getting-started/`}>
                <strong>Get started →</strong>
                <span>Add the plugin, create one mock file, and make your first request.</span>
            </a>
            <a class="card" href={`${base}/vite-plugin/`}>
                <strong>Learn the naming conventions →</strong>
                <span>See how URLs and HTTP methods resolve to local files.</span>
            </a>
            <a class="card" href={`${base}/proxy-panel/`}>
                <strong>Add optional browser controls →</strong>
                <span>Switch manifest scenarios or bypass individual endpoints.</span>
            </a>
        </div>

        <h2 id="how-a-request-is-resolved">How a request is resolved</h2>
        <p>
            Lockxy first respects source-controlled manifest decisions, then optional browser choices, and finally its
            automatic scenario and file-convention defaults. The
            <a href={`${base}/control-hierarchy/`}>control hierarchy</a> shows the complete decision flow and behavior
            matrix, including passthrough and manifest-controlled panel states.
        </p>
    </article>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
    .layer-section {
        margin: var(--wf-gap-xl) 0;
    }

    .layer-section > h2 {
        margin: var(--wf-gap-xl) 0 var(--wf-gap-m);
        padding-top: var(--wf-gap-s);
        font-size: var(--wf-font-size-xl);
    }

    .layer-section > p {
        max-width: 760px;
    }

    .layer-stack {
        display: grid;
        margin: var(--wf-gap-xl) 0;
        padding: 0;
        gap: var(--wf-gap-m);
        list-style: none;
    }

    .layer-stack li {
        display: flex;
        padding: var(--wf-gap-l);
        gap: var(--wf-gap-l);
        align-items: center;
        border: 1px solid var(--wf-docs-line);
        border-radius: 10px;
        background: var(--wf-docs-surface);
        box-shadow: 0 5px 18px var(--wf-docs-shadow-s);
    }

    .layer-copy {
        min-width: 0;
        flex: 1;
    }

    .layer-copy h3 {
        margin: 0 0 var(--wf-gap-xs);
        font-size: var(--wf-font-size-xl);
    }

    .layer-copy strong {
        display: block;
        font-size: var(--wf-font-size-l);
        line-height: 1.45;
    }

    .layer-stack p {
        margin: var(--wf-gap-s) 0 0;
    }

    .layer-illustration {
        width: clamp(7.5rem, 18vw, 8.75rem);
        height: auto;
        flex: 0 0 auto;
    }

    @media (max-width: 600px) {
        .layer-stack li {
            flex-direction: column;
            align-items: flex-start;
        }
    }
</style>
